{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 29, :comments ["<b>Those who curse you shall be cursed, and those who bless you shall be blessed.</b> And concerning Balaam it says (Numbers 24:9), 'Those who bless you shall be blessed, and those who curse you shall be cursed.' The righteous — their beginning is suffering and their end is tranquility, and so those who curse them precede those who bless them. And the wicked — their beginning is tranquility and their end is suffering; therefore Balaam put the blessing before the curse. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 27:29) from Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 66:4). And if so, why did HaShem say to Abraham (Genesis 12:3), 'And I will bless those who bless you, and him who curses you I will curse'? This is not difficult, for there He went back and said, 'and in you shall all the families of the earth be blessed' — and so the blessing is at the beginning and at the end. Or, for the reason that we explained there, He spoke in the language of the singular and the plural, for everyone shall bless him, and the individual who curses him shall be cursed."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 27, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:35:51.042828196Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}